<ExtractVariables name="EV-ModelResponseText" continueOnError="true" enabled="true">
  <DisplayName>Extract model response text for Model Armor</DisplayName>
  <!--
    Model Armor's UserPromptSource / ModelResponseSource accept a message
    template, and the obvious thing is a jsonPath() expression. That does NOT
    work here, and it fails silently in a way that then fails the call loudly.

    Proven from an Apigee debug session 2026-09-15 (rev 7):

      request.content                        len=3569  (the real body)
      $.contents[0].parts[0].text            None
      $.contents[-1].parts[-1].functionResponse.response  None   (policy default)

    So the body is present and the paths are correct, but the message-template
    jsonPath() function returns nothing against it. Three separate syntaxes
    were tried live and all resolved empty: the policy's own default with
    negative indices, a quoted path, and an unquoted path with [0]. An empty
    prompt is fatal to Model Armor rather than a skipped scan:

      500 steps.sanitize.user.prompt.FailedToExtractUserPrompt
      "Template was resolved to empty string"

    ExtractVariables uses a different JSONPath implementation and it demonstrably
    works in this same proxy (EV-ModelFromPath). So extraction happens here, in
    the policy whose job it is, and Model Armor just reads a flow variable.

    continueOnError is true: if a future request shape has no matching field,
    this must not fail the call on its own. Model Armor will still refuse an
    empty prompt, which is the behaviour we want, and the error then points at
    the real problem rather than at this policy.
  -->
  <Source>response</Source>
  <JSONPayload>
    <Variable name="response_text" type="string">
      <JSONPath>$.candidates[0].content.parts[0].text</JSONPath>
    </Variable>
  </JSONPayload>
  <VariablePrefix>llm</VariablePrefix>
  <IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>true</IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>
</ExtractVariables>
